A||||8||Black86SE||Sticking/hard to press Gas Pedal||||1||
Z||000000||Black86SE||09-13-2001||07:52 PM||||I was wondering if anybody has had problems with their gas pedal being hard to push. Sometimes mine will work just fine and other times I almost have to stomp on it to get it to go down. I don't know if it is the cable or the actual pedal itself. If anyone has had this problem and has fixed it let me know. NOTE: I have tried some spray on the pedal and on the throttle body.<P>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-13-2001||07:59 PM||||Most likely the cable. the pedal is not likely to bind even with major wear/damage. It's just a very simple lever on a pivot.<P>Could be the TB... check the holes the shaft runs in and check the walls of the TB for signs the butterfly is digging into it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||09-14-2001||08:42 PM||||mine does it too. mainly when engine is warm/hot. mine is because of a worn throttle body to butterfly relationship.||||reg||1
Z||000003||curtis||09-14-2001||11:08 PM||||My pedal was like that and starting to actually stick with the throttle open. I couldn't afford a new cable so I disconnected the end at the pedal assembly and curled it up so that it was sticking out the drivers window to hold it up in the air. Then I disconnected the other end at the throttle body. I poured 3in1 penetrating oil into both ends and worked the cable back and fourth. I let it sit for about 24hours periodically using more oil. After that the pedal was ten time easier and didn't stick at all. Hope this helps||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jncomutt||09-15-2001||01:25 AM||||I'm going to guess by your handle you have a V-6.  But for all the 4cyl folks that have had a problem where its hard to push at first, then sort of "Clicks" then is fine to move, The problem could lie in a simple adjustment.  On my 86 coupe, I noticed that the 'butterfly thing' was closing too far and 'locking' closed.  Theres an adjustment screw which will move out a 'Stop' for the butterfly.  I adjusted it out, so it wouldn't completly lock closed and that solved my problem.  Didn't affect the idle either for you are making a very minor adjustment, just to the point where it won't 'click'||||reg||1
Z||000005||isthiswhereiputausername?||09-15-2001||12:40 PM||||when the motors cold, spray the butterfly linkage and cable with penetrating oil, my wifes 2.8 in her tempest gets stiff sometimes and this solves it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black86SE||09-24-2001||03:15 PM||||Well, I disconnected the throttle cable from the throttle body and the pedal moves just fine. I then tried moving the TB by hand and it moves fine except when you let it snap back closed. Then it sticks when you try to open it again. I sprayed some WD-40 and some tri-flow with teflon on it but it doesn't make a difference. I powerwashed the engine compartment saturday and when I started to drive it the gas pedal was a lot harder to even push and hold. It made my foot and leg tired. I don't know what is up with that.<P>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-24-2001||07:46 PM||||Sounds like the throttle body needs work. The thing may be salvagable but don't count on it. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Black86SE||09-24-2001||10:13 PM||||I have a spare throttle body from an '85 GT. That should work right? It is still attatched to the plenum.<P>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS||||reg||1
